  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nToday's high temps fell shy of expectations as convection \nlingered longer than anticipated. Many thermometers northwest of\nthe Omaha metro fell shy of 80F. The last of the showers and \nthunderstorms dissipated and pushed into central Iowa this \nevening. They generally remained just under severe criteria, \nalthough we did get a handful of reports of 1\" hail / 60+ gusts.\nThe quiet didn't last long, as more convection has since\ndeveloped.\n\nSurface analysis reveals a warm front draped along the KS/NE state \nline. SPC mesoanalysis has highlighted a persistent instability \ngradient draped northwest to southeast across this CWA. Satellite \nimagery reminds us that Canadian wildfire smoke hangs heavy over the \nCWA (and a majority of the CONUS!) The upper pattern shows a \ndampened ridge over the Desert Southwest and shortwave pushing out \nof southeast Nebraska in the quasi-zonal flow north of that ridge. \n\nElevated thunderstorms have redeveloped in western Iowa and may\nexpand into the southern tier of counties of eastern Nebraska \nafter midnight when the LLJ pushes right into that stalled \nfront. Most storms should fall shy of severe criteria as the \nshear will be mostly below the inversion but there will be \nplenty of elevated instability (2500 J/kg) creating lots of\nlighting, thunder, heavy rain, and small hail. The chance of \nprecip at Omaha and Lincoln are below 15%. Convection should be \neast of the area by 10am.",
